====================================================================================================
(2) P2 — DOES THE LOCAL CONTEST BREAK FEWER RIGHT DECISIONS?  [CO-PRIMARY]
====================================================================================================
F-weak only, ONE-SIDED at alpha = 0.05. Among F-weak's contested, ruled,
before-RIGHT cells, is `broken | right` BELOW jd5-B's 167/622 = 26.8%?

jd5-B recomputed from its index: broken 167/622 26.8%, fixed 144/274 52.6% — matches the published 26.8% and 52.6%.

THIS COMPARISON IS UNPAIRED AND IT COMPARES TWO MECHANISMS. The contest object
(a finding vs the whole judgment), the ruling prompt, the before-state (the
findings judge's own derived verdict vs M0's) and the routing all moved between
the two rows. Fisher's exact test is the right SHAPE — jd5-B's 622 are data with
their own sampling error, and the one-sample binomial the questionnaire defaulted
to would call a four-point drop significant that a fair test calls borderline —
but no test here can attribute the difference to the decomposition alone. The
paired table at the end of this section is the one that holds the cell fixed, and
it is DESCRIPTIVE with its n.

