====================================================================================================
(5) THE PRE-REGISTERED READING
====================================================================================================
PREREG §4's four names were written down before either arm ran, so that no rule
is invented after the table:
  (A) P1 POSITIVE and P2 HOLDS       the first arm to net positive, by the
                                     hypothesised route
  (B) P2 HOLDS, P1 NULL or NEGATIVE  fewer right decisions broken and STILL not
                                     enough: f/b below a/(1-a)
  (C) P1 POSITIVE, P2 NOT SHOWN      nets positive by the FIX side, not the break
                                     side
  (D) neither

AND THE RULE THAT GOVERNS ALL FOUR: SPLITS ARE REPORTED AS SPLITS. A result that
is none of the four names — jd6 produced exactly one — is written down as the
split it is, with both tests' numbers, and is NOT rounded to whichever name it is
nearest. Nothing below is rounded.

P1  F-weak: fixed 276, broken 259, net +17 on 1639 cells, p = 0.4891 -> P1 is NULL
P2  F-weak: broken | right 259/670 38.7% against jd5-B's 167/622 = 26.8%, +11.8 pts, one-sided Fisher p = 1 -> P2 is NOT SHOWN

NAMED OUTCOME FOR F-WEAK: (D)
  NEITHER — P1 is NULL and P2 is not shown. The decomposition did not move the endpoint.

P1  F-strong: fixed 7, broken 11, net -4 on 1644 cells, p = 0.4807 -> P1 is NULL
  F-strong has NO P2: jd5-B's judge is Maverick, so only F-weak shares the
  comparator's model. Its P1 is a SEPARATE FAMILY at its own alpha = 0.05 and
  is never pooled with F-weak's; its rates sit beside F-weak's as descriptive.

Both arms' P1 read NULL. That is agreement about the endpoint and not
about the mechanism: the two judges differ in what they find, and section
(4d)'s splits are where that shows.

The descriptive paired table stands at n = 336: F-weak broke 82 that jd5-B kept and kept 41 that jd5-B broke, p = 0.0002756. It is not P2 and does not decide it.

WHAT THIS FILE DOES NOT CLAIM: nothing about jd3's P1, `single`/`self_critique`,
natural-error selection, or the same-model property (the findings judge also
rules on the appeals in both arms — accepted and disclosed, as jd3-jd6). No number
here is pooled with jd3-jd6's: the judgment form, the contest object and the
ruling prompt all differ.
